    <name>
      <text>Finding the Volume of the  cylinder-Q2</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Use the method of cylinders to determine the volume of the solid obtained by rotating the region bounded by \(y={@f_x@}, \, x={@lower_limit@}, \, x={@upper_limit@}\) and the \(\text{x-axis}\) about the \(\text{y-axis}\)</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Worked Solution</p>
<p>Here is a sketch of the bounded region with the axis of rotation shown,</p>
<p>{@plot(f_x,[x,lower_limit,upper_limit])@}</p>
<p> Recall that, by using the method of cylinders, the volume of the solid generated by the vertical lines \(x=a\) and \(x=b\), which is revolved about the \(\text{y-axis}\) (vertical axis of revolution) is given by</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle V= 2 \pi \int_{a}^{b}xf(x)dx\)</p>
<p>For this problem, we have \(a={@lower_limit@}, \, b={@upper_limit@}, \, y=f(x)={@f_x@}\) and radius \(r = x\). 
</p>
<p>Therefore,,</p>
<p>
\(
\begin{align}
\displaystyle V &= 2 \pi \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}x({@f_x@})dx \\
\displaystyle    &= 2 \pi \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}} {@x*f_x@}dx \\
                        &= {@int((2*%pi*x*f_x),x)@} \left.\begin{matrix}
 & \\ 
 & 
\end{matrix}\right|_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}} \\
    &= {@ta@}


\end{align}
\)
</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
